  volume    =  178,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "402--407",
  abstract  = "When randomized trials are not available to answer a causal
               question about the comparative effectiveness or safety of
               interventions, causal inferences are drawn using observational
               data. A helpful 2-step framework for causal inference from
               observational data is 1) specifying the protocol of the
               hypothetical randomized pragmatic trial that would answer the
               causal question of interest (the target trial), and 2) using the
               observational data to attempt to emulate that trial. The target
               trial framework can improve the quality of observational analyses
               by preventing some common biases. In this article, we discuss the
